#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f1baecc-44a1-5f72-ab09-9cd717b2aa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-156)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672553} {:answer/id #uuid "a625ebea-06cc-5b3c-8122-12f1ba7b7493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-202)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672565} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b9125a-b797-5012-84c0-7677861babcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(156-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672566} {:answer/id #uuid "a8207f34-a886-5676-97a7-86d9906b6cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(202-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672567} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1096c6-798b-5dee-b3be-0457fe09b40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(202-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672566} :answer-field/id #uuid "8253f107-8fbb-5332-bcf7-662b2191ff63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f3f105f-8a0c-5397-99cc-b07c91edc702" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $156$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=156\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(156-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672572 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21146af2-5811-55dc-806d-385ae7389530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-63)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672545} {:answer/id #uuid "52185f51-7b2b-5a1f-8289-22e102dd8d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(77-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672557} {:answer/id #uuid "b8161142-c7b3-5cc7-877f-cd3be70c5a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-77)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672568} {:answer/id #uuid "c84291f5-061a-557f-a30a-61f760e718be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(63-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672569} {:answer/id #uuid "ef261b24-85ae-5d42-8b83-24d63ccd2c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(77-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672557} :answer-field/id #uuid "8964b28f-24c7-5adb-ab5b-40b569e8a676" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4e2b098-d563-5151-aa29-2eb6c6d7ccda" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $77$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=77\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(77-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a256eb-8879-56ff-99df-300971473e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672700} {:answer/id #uuid "521072eb-dac5-5936-bb4c-e2735ce83f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672705} {:answer/id #uuid "83c6b4a3-aa03-5430-ab7c-03939204a89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672713} {:answer/id #uuid "95e22611-c985-54a7-b490-409d6b907071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672717} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaad396-e574-5c2a-8aa9-43bd73a75393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672729}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672729} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d11f1f-ed12-5f86-b3d9-4fa2bd52e0f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e82a4c1-ce90-57b8-bf6d-15ed1bc5fc36" :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Write the equation in terms of the given constant $k$." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1549dbdf-903f-520c-95cf-de4580ba5f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672696} {:answer/id #uuid "1df47aa2-06b7-5590-b63f-6c526362e681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672699} {:answer/id #uuid "50100a02-dd89-53f4-b9ea-de267bdc2ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672704} {:answer/id #uuid "89dc4e2b-c511-5563-b3cc-24a3ff4732dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/re